<!-- doc/src/sgml/copy-fixed.sgml -->

<sect1 id="copy-fixed" xreflabel="copy_fixed">
 <title>copy_fixed &mdash; fixed-width <command>COPY</command> format</title>

 <indexterm zone="copy-fixed">
  <primary>copy_fixed</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  The <filename>copy_fixed</filename> module adds a format called
  <literal>fixed</literal> to the <literal>FORMAT</literal> option of
  <xref linkend="sql-copy"/>.  In a fixed-width file every column occupies a
  predetermined number of bytes and is padded out to that width with a fill
  character; there is no delimiter between the columns, and no quoting or
  escaping of any kind.  Files of this shape are still the lingua franca of
  older systems, and this module lets
  <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> read and write them without an
  external conversion step:

<programlisting>
COPY employee TO '/tmp/employee.dat'
    WITH (format 'fixed', widths '5,10,10');
</programlisting>
 </para>

 <para>
  The module provides no SQL-accessible functions, so it is not installed with
  <command>CREATE EXTENSION</command>; nothing would cause the library to be
  loaded, and the <literal>fixed</literal> format would go on being
  unrecognized.  It has to be loaded into the server instead.  You can load it
  into an individual session:

<programlisting>
LOAD 'copy_fixed';
</programlisting>

  (You must be superuser to do that.)  More typical usage is to preload it
  into some or all sessions by including <literal>copy_fixed</literal> in
  <xref linkend="guc-session-preload-libraries"/> or
  <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/> in
  <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, so that the format is available to
  every session and to background processes.  In a session that has not loaded
  the library, <command>COPY</command> fails with
  <literal>COPY format "fixed" not recognized</literal>.
 </para>

 <important>
  <para>
   Field widths are counted in <emphasis>bytes</emphasis>, not in characters,
   and the bytes are those of the <emphasis>file</emphasis> encoding &mdash;
   the encoding named by the <literal>ENCODING</literal> option, which
   defaults to the client encoding, and which is not necessarily the server
   encoding.  A width of 10 means ten bytes in the file, whatever number of
   characters that turns out to be.  See <xref linkend="copy-fixed-widths"/>.
  </para>
 </important>

 <sect2 id="copy-fixed-options">
  <title>Options</title>

  <para>
   The <literal>fixed</literal> format accepts the following options in the
   <literal>WITH</literal> clause of <command>COPY</command>, alongside the
   standard options discussed in
   <xref linkend="copy-fixed-standard-options"/>.  All of them apply to
   <command>COPY TO</command> and <command>COPY FROM</command> alike, and all
   of them describe the file, so reading a file back requires the same
   settings that were used to write it.  As with the standard
   <command>COPY</command> options, writing one of them twice in the same
   command is an error rather than a case of the last one winning.
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry id="copy-fixed-widths">
    <term><literal>widths</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A comma-separated list of positive integers, such as
      <literal>'5,10,10'</literal>, giving the width of each field in the
      order the columns appear in the command.  This option is required;
      there is no sensible default for it, and a <command>COPY</command> that
      omits it fails.  The list must have exactly one entry per copied
      column, counting the columns of an explicit column list or of the query
      in a <literal>COPY (<replaceable>query</replaceable>) TO</literal>,
      not the columns of the table; a mismatch is reported when the
      <command>COPY</command> starts and says how many of each there were.
     </para>

     <para>
      Widths are measured in bytes of the file encoding.  This is the point
      about the format that most often surprises, so it is worth spelling out.
      If the file encoding is <literal>UTF8</literal>, the four-character
      string <literal>café</literal> occupies five bytes and needs a width of
      at least five.  If the same value is written to a
      <literal>LATIN1</literal> file, by giving
      <literal>ENCODING 'LATIN1'</literal>, it occupies four bytes and fits in
      a width of four.  Nothing about the server encoding enters into it: the
      value is converted to the file encoding first, and only then measured
      against the width.  Reading works the same way round, slicing the record
      into fields on file-encoding byte boundaries before converting anything.
      That is what makes the format able to read a file produced by a program
      that has never heard of the server's encoding, but it does mean that a
      width has to be chosen with the file's encoding in mind.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="copy-fixed-pad">
    <term><literal>pad</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The character used to pad a value out to its field width.  It must be a
      single one-byte ASCII character, and it may not be a
      newline or a carriage return.  The default is a space.
     </para>

     <para>
      On output, enough copies of this character are added to bring each field
      up to its width; on input, the same character is stripped from the
      padded end of each field before the value is handed to the column's
      input function.
     </para>

     <para>
      Stripping cannot tell padding from data, so a value whose own text ends
      with the padding character loses those characters when the file is read
      back under <literal>align 'left'</literal>, and one whose text begins
      with it loses them under <literal>align 'right'</literal>.  This holds
      at the default padding too: a <type>text</type> value of
      <literal>'ab&nbsp;&nbsp;'</literal> written into a field eight bytes
      wide comes back as <literal>'ab'</literal>, with no error and nothing to
      say that anything was dropped.  It is a property of the format rather
      than a defect in this implementation &mdash; a fixed-width layout
      records no boundary between a value and its padding &mdash; so a value
      that has to survive a round trip exactly cannot rely on its leading or
      trailing padding characters surviving with it.
     </para>

     <para>
      <command>COPY FROM</command> accepts only a space here, and rejects any
      other padding character before it reads anything.  Stripping padding
      that could also be part of a value cannot be done unambiguously, and the
      result would be silently wrong data rather than an error; see
      <xref linkend="copy-fixed-limitations"/>.  <command>COPY TO</command>
      accepts any padding character, which is what a setting such as
      <literal>pad '0'</literal> is for: producing a file in the layout some
      other system expects.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="copy-fixed-align">
    <term><literal>align</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Which end of the field the value is placed at, either
      <literal>left</literal> (the default) or <literal>right</literal>.  With
      <literal>left</literal> the value starts at the beginning of the field
      and the padding follows it; with <literal>right</literal> the padding
      comes first and the value ends at the end of the field, which is the
      conventional layout for numeric columns.  On input the setting says
      which end the padding is stripped from.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="copy-fixed-record-delimiter">
    <term><literal>record_delimiter</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      What separates one record from the next: <literal>lf</literal> (the
      default) for a single newline, <literal>crlf</literal> for a carriage
      return followed by a newline, or <literal>none</literal> for records
      written end to end with nothing between them, which is possible here
      precisely because every record is the same length.
     </para>

     <para>
      Note that this is deliberately explicit rather than platform-dependent.
      The text and <literal>CSV</literal> formats terminate lines in a file
      with whatever the platform uses, which means a <command>COPY TO</command>
      file written on Windows gets <literal>CRLF</literal> and the same
      command on any other platform gets <literal>LF</literal>.  A
      fixed-width file is usually being exchanged with some other system, and
      what that system expects has nothing to do with what the server happens
      to be running on, so the choice is left to the user and defaults to
      <literal>lf</literal> everywhere.
     </para>

     <para>
      On input, the delimiter is checked rather than searched for: a record is
      read as exactly the number of bytes the widths add up to, and the
      delimiter must follow.  If it does not, the <command>COPY</command>
      fails instead of quietly reading the rest of the file out of step.  A
      final record with no delimiter after it is accepted, as it is in the
      text format.
     </para>

     <para>
      Do not feed a <literal>record_delimiter 'none'</literal> file through
      <command>COPY ... FROM STDIN</command> in
      <application>psql</application>.  The <command>COPY</command> can report
      success and load nothing but corrupted rows.  Two typed lines of a
      five-byte-per-record layout, for instance, load as three rows in which
      every value is misaligned, and no error is raised at any point.
     </para>

     <para>
      The cause is that <application>psql</application> has to read the data a
      line at a time to find the <literal>\.</literal> that ends it, and it
      sends each line with the newline that terminated it.  Where the format
      expects no delimiter those newlines are data, so every record after the
      first is shifted by one byte for each line that preceded it.  When the
      total number of bytes is not a multiple of the record width the
      <command>COPY</command> does end with a complaint that the data stopped
      in the middle of a record, but that is luck rather than detection: as
      soon as the count happens to divide evenly, the misaligned records are
      accepted in silence.  Such a file has to be read with
      <command>COPY ... FROM</command> naming the file, or through a client
      that sends the data unaltered.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="copy-fixed-standard-options">
  <title>Interaction with the Standard <command>COPY</command> Options</title>

  <para>
   The <literal>fixed</literal> format honors those standard
   <command>COPY</command> options that are meaningful for it:
   <literal>NULL</literal>, <literal>DEFAULT</literal>,
   <literal>ENCODING</literal>, <literal>ON_ERROR</literal>,
   <literal>LOG_VERBOSITY</literal>, <literal>REJECT_LIMIT</literal> and
   <literal>FREEZE</literal>.  They behave exactly as
   <xref linkend="sql-copy"/> describes them; in particular a row whose field
   cannot be converted is skipped or nulled under
   <literal>ON_ERROR</literal> just as it would be in the text format, and the
   error context names the record and the column.
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>NULL</literal> marker deserves a closer look, because its
   default here is not the one the built-in formats use.  For the
   <literal>fixed</literal> format the default marker is the
   <emphasis>empty string</emphasis>: a NULL is written as a field containing
   nothing but padding, and on input a field that is nothing but padding is
   read back as NULL.  That is the natural spelling of a missing value in a
   fixed-width layout, and it makes a file round-trip through
   <command>COPY TO</command> and <command>COPY FROM</command> unchanged.
   The server does not supply this default: it deliberately leaves
   <literal>NULL</literal> unset for a format it does not know, and
   <filename>copy_fixed</filename> chooses the empty string for itself.  A
   different marker can of course be given, as in
   <literal>NULL '\N'</literal>, in which case that text is what is written
   and matched, still padded out to the field width; a marker that is longer
   than a field it has to be written into is an error.
  </para>

  <para>
   Because the default <literal>NULL</literal> marker is the empty string, an
   empty string and a NULL are the same thing in a
   <literal>fixed</literal> file unless a different marker is chosen.  It also
   means <literal>DEFAULT ''</literal> collides with the NULL marker and is
   rejected, as an explicit <literal>NULL '<replaceable>x</replaceable>'
   DEFAULT '<replaceable>x</replaceable>'</literal> would be.
  </para>

  <para>
   The remaining standard options describe things a fixed-width file does not
   have, and specifying any of them is an error rather than being silently
   ignored: <literal>DELIMITER</literal> (fields are found by counting bytes,
   not by looking for a separator), <literal>QUOTE</literal>,
   <literal>ESCAPE</literal>, <literal>FORCE_QUOTE</literal>,
   <literal>FORCE_NOT_NULL</literal>, <literal>FORCE_NULL</literal> and
   <literal>FORCE_ARRAY</literal> (there is no quoting and no array
   structure), and <literal>HEADER</literal> (see
   <xref linkend="copy-fixed-limitations"/>).
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="copy-fixed-limitations">
  <title>Limitations</title>

  <para>
   Field widths are counted in bytes of the file encoding, never in
   characters.  There is no character-based width unit, and adding one would
   be a different format rather than an option: the whole point of a
   fixed-width file is that a given column always begins at a given byte
   offset, which a character count does not pin down for any encoding whose
   characters differ in length.
  </para>

  <para>
   A value that does not fit in its field is an error; it is never truncated.
   Truncating would produce a file that looks well-formed and silently
   contains wrong data, which is a considerably worse outcome than a
   <command>COPY</command> that stops and reports the column, the number of
   bytes the value needed, and the width it was given.  The same applies to a
   <literal>NULL</literal> marker that is too long for a field it has to be
   written into, which is reported as its own error so that it is not confused
   with an oversized value.
  </para>

  <para>
   With any <literal>record_delimiter</literal> other than
   <literal>none</literal>, a value that contains a newline or a carriage
   return is rejected on output.  A fixed-width file has no escaping
   mechanism, so such a value would be written literally, split the record
   across physical lines, and produce a file that this same format could not
   read back.  Refusing to write it is the only way to keep the file readable.
   Under <literal>record_delimiter 'none'</literal> there is no line framing
   to break, and the value is written as it stands.  For the same reason, a
   <literal>NULL</literal> marker containing a newline or a carriage return is
   rejected outright.
  </para>

  <para>
   On input, a null byte (that is, a byte of zero) anywhere in a field is
   rejected, with an error naming the column it turned up in.  In a
   fixed-width file a null byte is a byte like any other, and files written by
   older systems do sometimes use it as filler, but a field reaches the
   column's input function as a C string, so everything from the null byte to
   the end of the field would be quietly dropped.  Reading in a value that has
   been silently truncated is worse than refusing to read the file, so such a
   file has to be filtered before it can be loaded.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>COPY FROM</command> requires <literal>pad</literal> to be a space.
   The padding of a field is not marked in the file in any way, so reading a
   field means stripping the padding character from one end of it for as long
   as it is found, and that can only be right when the character cannot be
   part of a value.  Under <literal>pad '0'</literal>, for instance, the value
   <literal>100</literal> in a field five bytes wide is written
   <literal>10000</literal> and read back as <literal>1</literal>, and a
   right-aligned <literal>0</literal> in the same field is written
   <literal>00000</literal>, strips to nothing, and matches the default
   <literal>NULL</literal> marker.  No rule for where to stop stripping helps,
   because <literal>00100</literal>, <literal>100</literal> and
   <literal>0000100</literal> are the same bytes in such a file; the original
   value simply is not recoverable.  <command>COPY FROM</command> therefore
   refuses the setting rather than quietly returning wrong values.  A file
   padded with something else can still be produced with
   <command>COPY TO</command>, and can be read back after being converted to
   space padding by other means.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>pad</literal> and <literal>align</literal> apply to every column
   of the <command>COPY</command>.  There is no per-column form of either, so
   a file that pads its text columns on the right and its numeric columns on
   the left &mdash; a common enough convention &mdash; cannot be produced or
   read in one command.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>HEADER</literal> is not supported in either direction.  A header
   line does not fit the layout in any obvious way: column names would have to
   be truncated to their field widths or spill past them, and on input a
   header would have to be told apart from a data record that happens to
   contain the same bytes.
  </para>
 </sect2>

 <sect2 id="copy-fixed-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Set up a table to work with:

<programlisting>
LOAD 'copy_fixed';

CREATE TABLE employee (id int, name text, salary numeric);
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1, 'Alice', 4500.00),
                            (22, 'Bob', 300.5),
                            (333, NULL, 12345.67);
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Writing it out in fields of 5, 10 and 10 bytes.  A visible padding
   character is used here to make the layout easy to see; the default is a
   space:

<programlisting>
COPY employee TO STDOUT WITH (format 'fixed', widths '5,10,10', pad '.');
</programlisting>

<screen>
1....Alice.....4500.00...
22...Bob.......300.5.....
333............12345.67..
</screen>

   Every line is 25 bytes plus a newline, and the third row's NULL name has
   become a field of nothing but padding, which is what the default
   <literal>NULL</literal> marker &mdash; the empty string &mdash; amounts to.
  </para>

  <para>
   The same rows right-aligned, with an explicit <literal>NULL</literal>
   marker:

<programlisting>
COPY employee TO STDOUT WITH (format 'fixed', widths '5,10,10',
                              align 'right', null '(none)');
</programlisting>

<screen>
    1     Alice   4500.00
   22       Bob     300.5
  333    (none)  12345.67
</screen>
  </para>

  <para>
   Reading a file back needs the same settings that produced it, and, since
   <command>COPY FROM</command> takes only space padding, a file meant to be
   read back has to be written with the default padding.  Below are the same
   rows right-aligned and padded with spaces, and with the default
   <literal>NULL</literal> marker, so the third row's name is an empty field.
   That NULL comes back as a NULL, so <application>psql</application> is asked
   to display one distinguishably:

<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE employee_in (id int, name text, salary numeric);

\pset null (null)

COPY employee_in FROM STDIN WITH (format 'fixed', widths '5,10,10',
                                  align 'right');
    1     Alice   4500.00
   22       Bob     300.5
  333            12345.67
\.

SELECT * FROM employee_in ORDER BY id;
</programlisting>

<screen>
 id  |  name  |  salary
-----+--------+----------
   1 | Alice  |  4500.00
  22 | Bob    |    300.5
 333 | (null) | 12345.67
(3 rows)
</screen>
  </para>

  <para>
   Widths are byte counts, which shows up as soon as a value is not pure
   ASCII.  In a <literal>UTF8</literal> database, and
   writing a <literal>UTF8</literal> file, the four-character string
   <literal>café</literal> takes five of the six bytes of its field and leaves
   room for exactly one padding byte:

<programlisting>
COPY (SELECT 'café' AS word, 1 AS n) TO STDOUT
    WITH (format 'fixed', widths '6,3', pad '.');
</programlisting>

<screen>
café.1..
</screen>
  </para>

  <para>
   A value that does not fit is refused rather than truncated:

<programlisting>
COPY employee TO STDOUT WITH (format 'fixed', widths '5,4,10', pad '.');
</programlisting>

<screen>
ERROR:  value too long for fixed-width field
DETAIL:  Column "name" needs 5 bytes, but its field width is 4.
</screen>
  </para>
 </sect2>

</sect1>
